So, how can we create the network that can approximate this function in the first place?\
\
The 
\f1\b \ul Bellman Equation
\f0\b0 \ulnone , states that the 
\f1\b value, 
\f0\b0 or
\f1\b  reward 
\f0\b0 of an action
\f1\b  
\f0\b0 (taken at a certain state) can be expressed as some function of the 
\f1\b immediate
\f0\b0  reward, and the 
\f1\b expected 
\f0\b0 reward of the 
\f1\b next state we end up in 
\f0\b0 (this value is dependent on gamma, or the discount rate).\
\
All Q-functions, obey this law. So, this allows us to find the 
\f1\b difference 
\f0\b0 between
\f1\b  
\f2\i our
\f1\i0  version of the Q-function, 
\f0\b0 and
\f1\b  the actual Q-function
\f0\b0  we are trying to find.\
\
