\cf2 The actual Q-function? How do we know that? Isn\'92t this what we\'92re trying to find?\
Actually, we 
\f3\i don\'92t
\f0\i0  know the actual Q-function. That\'92s why we need to approximate it. However, the Bellman Equation allows us to create a \'93target point\'94, that 
\f3\i our
\f0\i0  Q-function is constantly working towards.\
\
For any state-action pair (s, a); a target value can be create through:\
The immediate reward, 
\f8\i R
\f0\i0 , we get from taking 
\f8\i a
\f0\i0  in 
\f8\i s
\f0\i0 , and;\
The maximum Q-value for the preceding state, 
\f8\i s\'92
\f0\i0 \
Thus,\

\f6 target = r + \uc0\u947  * max(Q(s', a'))
\f0 \
